        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-8827144e9c2ae23a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"The embedded “Je t’aime!” represents a straightforward avowal of loving feeling. Its imagined power to transform the ashamed prince is a perlocutionary fairy-tale effect, not a relational undertaking, and there is no independent evidence that the words function as reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The phrase is simultaneously a generic fairy-tale quotation and Cyrano’s revoicing of Roxane’s immediately preceding declaration, but either construal gives it predominantly truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Cyrano embeds the formula in a generic fairy-tale scenario (“when one says ‘I love you’”), while also echoing and reformulating Roxane’s direct “Je vous aime” addressed to him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"After Roxane declares “Je vous aime, vivez!”, Cyrano revoices her declaration as the fairy-tale words spoken to a shame-filled, ugly prince. He contrasts their miraculous effect in stories with his own unchanged appearance. This self-deprecating framing and the words’ hoped-for emotional impact do not change the represented love formula from an avowal into a commitment or expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“lorsqu’on dit: Je t’aime ! au prince plein de honte”\",\"supports\":\"The love formula is explicitly embedded as speech to a recipient and represents an avowal of love rather than a direct declaration by Cyrano.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane immediately says, “Je vous aime, vivez !” before Cyrano’s reply.\",\"supports\":\"Cyrano’s quoted formula revoices Roxane’s direct avowal, strengthening the truth-conditional reading.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Il sent sa laideur fondre à ces mots de soleil”\",\"supports\":\"The represented words have a transformative fairy-tale effect, but that effect is perlocutionary and does not establish performative commitment or expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Mais tu t’apercevrais que je reste pareil.”\",\"supports\":\"Cyrano applies the fairy-tale comparison to Roxane and himself while denying that her words can literally transform him; this is contextual framing rather than ontology failure.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the represented avowal. The fairy-tale quotation, revoicing, and imagined transformative effect are adequately handled as utterance status and context; they create no residual core force requiring E, P, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
